/// The kernel's label-keyed Messenger policy (application package system,
/// phase 1; `docs/architecture/ipc-security.md`).
///
/// A process stamped with a label (`app:<reverse.dns.name>` or
/// `system:<name>`) is default-deny: it may only use what its own namespace
/// grants (`app.<id>.<name>` services, `app/<id>/...` topics) plus the allow
/// rules loaded here. `LoadLabel` is the one way rules get in: it is the
/// request body of the privileged `acl_load` messenger op, which needs
/// `CAP_IPC_CONTROL`, and it is never a parcel any service receives.
interface os.lazy.messenger.policy.v1 {
    /// Replace every rule of `label` with `rules` (first match wins, anything
    /// unmatched is denied). An empty list revokes the label's grants. The
    /// label is interned if new; a malformed label or more rules than the
    /// kernel stores per label (256) fails with `EINVAL`.
    ///
    /// `program` is for a child label only (`app:<id>/<name>`, issue #832): the
    /// absolute path of the executable that takes it. A task labelled
    /// `app:<id>` that `execve`s exactly that path becomes `app:<id>/<name>`,
    /// and may not start programs itself. A Messenger call by the child
    /// needs the intersection of these rules and the app's own; the file
    /// and `AF_INET` gates of a confined label use the child's own rule ids
    /// (its file ids and the confine marker), which the app's loaded rules
    /// must hold too, the marker apart. The app's label must already be
    /// interned (`ENOENT` otherwise); `program` on any other label, a path
    /// that is not absolute and canonical, or a child label with rules but no
    /// program is `EINVAL`; every allow rule of the child must be one the
    /// app's loaded rules allow (`EACCES`: it would widen the app). Loading a
    /// child with no rules and no `program` revokes it and drops the binding,
    /// and so does revoking the app (an empty list for `app:<id>`).
    method LoadLabel(label: String = 1, rules: Array<LabelRule> = 2, program: Option<String> = 3) -> ();

    /// One rule. `interface_id` and `method` accept the wildcards
    /// `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` and `0xFFFFFFFF`; `allow` of `false` is an
    /// explicit deny that shadows later rules.
    struct LabelRule {
        interface_id: U64 = 1,
        method: U32 = 2,
        allow: Bool = 3
    }
}

/// Kernel ACL scope for resolving a service name. The kernel evaluates a
/// labelled task's `Resolve` of `name` against its rules on this interface id,
/// using `fnv1a32(name)` as the method id, so a rule grants one exact name.
/// Only the interface id is contractual; nothing ever serves this interface.
interface os.lazy.messenger.names.resolve.v1 {
    /// Check that the actor may resolve `name`.
    method Resolve(name: String = 1) -> ();
}

/// Kernel ACL scope for spawning a child into a development label (issue #529,
/// `docs/lazyrad-package-plan.md` section 3). A labelled task (an IDE) may give
/// a child it spawns the label `dev:<system_name>` only when its own rules
/// allow this interface id with `fnv1a32(<the dev label>)` as the method, and
/// only while `pkgd` holds an approved rule set for that label. The child keeps
/// the caller's uid, gid and session, and never gains a capability. A manifest
/// asks for it with `develop = true`, which compiles to the wildcard method.
/// Only the interface id is contractual; nothing ever serves this interface.
interface os.lazy.process.label.spawn.v1 {
    /// Check that the actor may spawn a child labelled `label`.
    method Assign(label: String = 1) -> ();
}
